    <h2>Industrial Pay, Older Homes, Different Work</h2>

    <p>Decatur's economy doesn't run on engineering salaries. It runs on industrial wages — solid, steady, often dual-income, with predictable union and shift patterns. Decatur Morgan Hospital is the single largest employer at around 1,900 people. Decatur City Schools employ another 1,445. GE Appliances under the Haier ownership runs roughly 1,400 people across the east-side plant after the 2018 expansion. Morgan County Schools, Wayne-Sanderson Farms, 3M, Nucor Steel, Daikin America, Hyosung, Toray Composite Materials, the United Launch Alliance facility — each adds hundreds more.</p>

    <p>One important note for any HVAC contractor reading this: Daikin America in Decatur is a fluorochemical plant — refrigerant gases and fluoropolymers — not an HVAC equipment plant. The parent company makes HVAC equipment globally, but this site has nothing to do with that. Don't write your marketing around the assumption that local homeowners are Daikin-brand-loyal because of the plant. They aren't.</p>

    <p>What the industrial economy means for trade work: Decatur is a workhorse market. Less premium upsell than Huntsville or Madison. More cash work, more replacement of original mid-century systems, more rental-property landlord demand in the inner-loop bungalow neighborhoods. The customers value getting the job done right at a fair price more than they value the highest-end product tier. That changes how a contractor's website should be framed — the trust signals are different, the pricing language is different, and the photo gallery should look different.</p>

    <h2>The Historic Districts and What They Demand</h2>

    <p>Decatur has the largest concentration of Victorian-era Craftsman and bungalow homes in the state of Alabama. This is not marketing hyperbole — it's the practical reason Old Decatur and Albany are both on the National Register and both have local design-review boards. Old Decatur runs along Bank Street near the river with Victorian and Italianate stock from the late 1800s, 116-plus acres of historic district. Albany sits northwest of downtown, a separate city until the 1927 merger, with Queen Anne, Colonial Revival, American Four Square, and Dutch Colonial Revival stock from the planned-town 1887 founding, all surrounding Delano Park.</p>

    <p>Trade work in those districts is different in ways that matter to a website. Cast-iron drain lines are 100-plus years old and failing systematically. Original 60-amp and 100-amp split-bus electrical panels need upgrading — and knob-and-tube wiring remnants still exist in the oldest Albany homes. Original sill plates and crawlspace conditions create heightened termite risk. Period-correct exterior paint requires specific protocols and approved color palettes through design review. Foundation work on original brick piers and pier-and-beam construction is constrained by what design review will allow to be visible.</p>

    <p>The homeowners in those districts know all of this. They've often owned the house for ten or twenty years, they've dealt with three or four contractors who didn't understand the rules, and they're now searching specifically for someone who does. A website that mentions historic-district experience — knob-and-tube remediation, design-review-compliant work, period-appropriate restoration — wins those customers. A generic contractor site loses them in the first thirty seconds.</p>

    <h2>The River, the Refuge, and the Geography That Shapes the Work</h2>

    <p>Decatur sits on the south bank of the Tennessee River in Morgan County. The river isn't background scenery — it actively shapes the economy and the trade work. Heavy barge traffic. Riverfront industrial sites at Nucor and 3M. Wheeler Lake to the east is a Tennessee River impoundment that defines the eastern edge of the city. Wheeler National Wildlife Refuge sweeps 35,000 acres east of town. Flint Creek cuts north-to-south and separates Indian Hills and Burningtree from the central city. Point Mallard Park spans 750 acres along the river with a water park, golf course, and ice rink.</p>

    <p>What that geography means for contractors: the riverfront subdivisions on the north edge of the city have high water tables that complicate basement and crawlspace work. The Flint Creek and Wheeler Refuge proximity drives heightened mosquito-control and wildlife-control demand. Riverfront and bluff properties have specific erosion-control, bulkhead, and algae-and-lichen issues that standard pressure-washing playbooks don't address. The light-industrial Beltline corridor and the rail-corridor neighborhoods create commercial-pest-control B2B demand that doesn't exist in Madison or Athens. A contractor's website that ignores all of that and just says "we serve Decatur" leaves real revenue on the table.</p>

    <h2>Where the Decatur Work Actually Sits</h2>

    <p>Old Decatur and Albany — the historic districts — drive the high-touch restoration and design-review-compliant work. The premium tier of Decatur's market.</p>

    <p>Burningtree — south Decatur, wooded golf-course community on larger lots — is the upper-tier subdivision market. Long driveways, mature trees, generator installs, larger seasonal landscape projects. Indian Hills, across Flint Creek from the central city, runs in a similar tier with a distinct rural-edge character.</p>

    <p>Pointe Mallard Estates, Stratford Place and Stratford Village, Timberland Lake Estates, and the established east-side subdivisions are the established mid-tier. 1980s-1990s construction, family-heavy, the broad volume market for replacement HVAC and roofing and routine maintenance work.</p>

    <p>The Modaus Road corridor, Somerville Road corridor, and the Beltline-era 1960s-1980s subdivisions are the meat-and-potatoes service area of the city — the volume center for driveway replacement, ranch-stock HVAC service, original-system replacement work across plumbing, electrical, and mechanical trades.</p>

    <p>West Decatur and the inner-loop bungalow neighborhoods carry the highest rental-property density and bungalow-stock turnover. Tenant-turnover handyman work, landlord-paid repairs, lower-ticket but higher-volume. The Country Club / Stone Acres area west of downtown holds an older established upper-mid neighborhood with larger lots near the country club.</p>

